{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\nThe sandbox enforcement tests added by `e668-b496-e264-4283` in\n`tests/scripts/test_mutation_sandbox.py` assert only that a file is ABSENT after\nrunning a wrapped subprocess:\n\n```python\nassert not target.exists()          # denial \"proven\"\n```\n\nNothing asserts the sandboxed child actually launched and reached the write attempt.\nIf `sandbox-exec`/`bwrap` fails to start at all — bad profile, missing binary, a\nnamespace refusal, an argv regression — the write never happens, the file is absent,\nand the test PASSES. A total failure of the sandbox wrapper is indistinguishable from\ncorrect enforcement.\n\n`test_the_incident_shape_rm_rf_is_denied` has the same shape in reverse: it asserts a\npre-existing `keep.txt` still exists. A sandbox launch failure trivially leaves the file\nin place, producing a false pass.\n\n## Why this matters more than a normal test gap\n\nThese are the ONLY tests that demonstrate the sandbox works. `e668`'s acceptance\ncriteria deliberately required observed enforcement rather than argv assertions,\nprecisely so the control could not be satisfied by construction alone. A test that\npasses when the mechanism is absent silently reintroduces the weakness those criteria\nwere written to exclude — the evidence for the whole feature rests on it.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] Every enforcement test asserts positive LIVENESS of the sandboxed child through\n      an artifact the CHILD ITSELF produced after starting — a sentinel it wrote to an\n      ALLOWED path, or a marker it printed to stdout — asserted before the denial is\n      read. The wrapper's exit status does NOT qualify: a sandbox that fails to launch\n      also exits non-zero, so exit code cannot distinguish executed-and-denied from\n      never-ran, which is the exact ambiguity this ticket removes.\n- [x] The liveness assertion is proven load-bearing: with the sandbox mechanism forced\n      unavailable so the child never launches, the test goes RED rather than green.\n      Demonstrated by real substitution, not asserted.\n- [x] `test_the_incident_shape_rm_rf_is_denied` gains the same liveness check, so a\n      launch failure cannot pass as \"the target survived\".\n\n## Acceptance Criteria — blast radius\n\nAn enforcement test asserts that a control DENIES something. If the control silently\ndoes nothing, the denied action HAPPENS — which is the incident's own mechanism,\nre-created inside the test suite. These tests must therefore be fail-safe: a total\nsandbox failure must produce a red test, never damage.\n\n- [x] Write-denial is proven with a CREATE, not a DELETE, wherever the proof does not\n      require deletion. A leaked create is inert; a leaked delete destroys. Verified by\n      inspection: no enforcement test removes anything except the single incident-shape\n      test below.\n- [x] The incident-shape test is bounded so a TOTAL sandbox failure cannot escape the\n      pytest tmp dir. The target is passed via the environment and dereferenced as\n      `\"${VAR:?}\"`, so an empty or unset variable ABORTS the shell instead of expanding\n      to `rm -rf /*` — the exact guard whose absence caused the 2026-08-26 incident —\n      and the path is never interpolated into the shell string.\n- [x] Python refuses to launch the destructive child unless the target is non-empty,\n      absolute, and strictly inside `tmp_path`. Verified by a test asserting the\n      guard raises when handed a path outside `tmp_path`.\n- [x] The `${VAR:?}` guard is itself proven, not assumed: with the variable set empty\n      the shell aborts non-zero and the canary survives, asserted by a test that runs\n      that case WITHOUT a sandbox — so it demonstrates the guard alone is sufficient to\n      stop the incident's expansion.\n\n## Definition of Done\n\n- No enforcement test in the file can pass when the sandbox fails to launch.\n- Each liveness check is shown RED under a forced launch failure.\n- No enforcement test can damage anything outside the pytest tmp dir even if the\n  sandbox enforces NOTHING at all.\n- `make lint`, `make typecheck`.\n\n## Notes\n\nFound by the Gerrit `LLM-Review` bot on change 2256 (patch sets 4 and 7; advisory, so\nit did not block the merge). Discovered from `e668-b496-e264-4283`."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787882417943525001,"uuid":"6d5fc31e-4bfd-4634-b350-f245c8e401b2"}